const EXAM_DATA={"version":"2026-11-13-g9-grammar-original-unit-patch-v2","models":[{"model":"A","grade":9,"minutes":120,"marks":40,"theme":"Practical community skills","tasks":[{"id":"g9-A-L1","skill":"listening","group":1,"title":"Listening 1 — Choose the picture","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"Where will the club meet?","choices":["school","library","sports hall"],"images":["images/illustration-1-1.png","images/illustration-1-2.png","images/illustration-1-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"Our environment club is planning its next meeting. We usually meet at school, but the hall is being repaired. Someone suggested the sports centre, but it closes too early. This Thursday, we will use the public library, where a quiet room has already been booked."},{"stem":"Which hobby did the speaker choose?","choices":["art palette","football","camera"],"images":["images/illustration-2-1.png","images/illustration-2-2.png","images/illustration-2-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"I wanted to try something creative after school. My brother plays football, and my cousin takes photographs. I enjoy both, but this term I have joined an art club. I have bought a paintbrush and a small palette because we are learning watercolour painting."},{"stem":"What weather does the latest forecast predict?","choices":["sunny","rainy","snowy"],"images":["images/illustration-3-1.png","images/illustration-3-2.png","images/illustration-3-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"We are planning a class trip tomorrow, so everyone has checked the weather. Yesterday, the forecast showed sunshine, and one website even mentioned cold winds. This morning, however, the updated forecast says there will be rain for most of the day. Remember to bring a jacket."},{"stem":"What is the confirmed time of the event?","choices":["3 oclock","7 oclock","5 oclock"],"images":["images/illustration-4-1.png","images/illustration-4-2.png","images/illustration-4-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"Please note a change to the community festival programme. Some posters showed three o'clock, and an early email said seven o'clock. The organisers have now confirmed that the opening performance will begin at five o'clock. Please arrive at least fifteen minutes before the show."},{"stem":"What is the family's weekend plan?","choices":["beach","camping","shopping"],"images":["images/illustration-5-1.png","images/illustration-5-2.png","images/illustration-5-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"My family discussed what to do this weekend. My sister wanted to go shopping, and my father suggested spending the afternoon at the beach. We finally decided to go camping in the mountains, because everyone wanted to spend the night outdoors and watch the stars."}],"audio":"audio/g9-a-listening-1.mp3","transcript":"Text 1. Our environment club is planning its next meeting. We usually meet at school, but the hall is being repaired. Someone suggested the sports centre, but it closes too early. This Thursday, we will use the public library, where a quiet room has already been booked.\n\nText 2. I wanted to try something creative after school. My brother plays football, and my cousin takes photographs. I enjoy both, but this term I have joined an art club. I have bought a paintbrush and a small palette because we are learning watercolour painting.\n\nText 3. We are planning a class trip tomorrow, so everyone has checked the weather. Yesterday, the forecast showed sunshine, and one website even mentioned cold winds. This morning, however, the updated forecast says there will be rain for most of the day. Remember to bring a jacket.\n\nText 4. Please note a change to the community festival programme. Some posters showed three o'clock, and an early email said seven o'clock. The organisers have now confirmed that the opening performance will begin at five o'clock. Please arrive at least fifteen minutes before the show.\n\nText 5. My family discussed what to do this weekend. My sister wanted to go shopping, and my father suggested spending the afternoon at the beach. We finally decided to go camping in the mountains, because everyone wanted to spend the night outdoors and watch the stars.","instructions":"Listen to five short texts. For each text, choose the correct picture (A, B or C).","naturalAudioAvailable":true},{"id":"g9-A-L2","skill":"listening","group":2,"title":"Listening 2 — Interview","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"Where was the first episode recorded?","choices":["the library","the sports hall","the old science classroom"],"answer":2},{"stem":"When did the team start?","choices":["September","January","October"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Who may apply?","choices":["teachers only","Grade Twelve only","Grades Eight and Nine"],"answer":2},{"stem":"When do they meet?","choices":["Tuesday after school","Friday morning","Sunday evening"],"answer":0},{"stem":"What skill did they learn?","choices":["checking facts","painting walls","playing football"],"answer":0}],"audio":"audio/g9-a-listening-2.mp3","transcript":"Interviewer: Welcome to Student Voices. Today we are speaking with Maha about the community radio project. Why was this project started?\n\nMaha: We wanted to help our school in a practical way, not just read about problems. Students suggested ideas, and our teachers helped us organise a small team.\n\nInterviewer: Tell us about your first activity.\n\nMaha: The first episode was recorded in the old science classroom. We chose something manageable and listened to feedback from other students.\n\nInterviewer: When did everything begin?\n\nMaha: The team began in October. We prepared the materials together and agreed on simple responsibilities.\n\nInterviewer: Who can get involved?\n\nMaha: Students from Grades Eight and Nine can apply. Everyone needs to show respect and take turns.\n\nInterviewer: When does the group meet?\n\nMaha: We meet every Tuesday after school. We try to finish each session with a useful plan for the next one.\n\nInterviewer: What is the most important lesson?\n\nMaha: We learned how to check a story before sharing it. It has also helped us become more confident when explaining our ideas.\n\nInterviewer: Would you recommend joining?\n\nMaha: Yes. Try something small, listen to others, and keep improving. We are proud that young people can lead meaningful changes, even when resources are limited and progress takes time. Thank you for inviting me.","instructions":"Listen to the interview twice. Choose the correct answer (A, B or C).","naturalAudioAvailable":true},{"id":"g9-A-GV","skill":"grammar_vocabulary","group":1,"title":"Grammar & Vocabulary — One cloze","marks":10,"passage":"Everyone (1) ____ an identity. My friend Huda usually (2) ____ stories with her grandmother, but today she (3) ____ a video about her family. We (4) ____ proud of our languages and customs. Huda is very (5) ____ when she speaks to visitors. She (6) ____ her ideas and never (7) ____ anyone unfairly. At the moment, her classmates (8) ____ questions about the video. They want to know how traditions (9) ____ people together. Huda believes that everyone should (10) ____ their own interests while respecting others.","items":[{"stem":"Gap 1","choices":["had","has","have","having"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 2","choices":["shares","share","shared","sharing"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 3","choices":["is making","made","make","makes"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 4","choices":["was","be","are","is"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 5","choices":["confide","confident","confidence","confidently"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 6","choices":["explains","explain","explaining","explained"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 7","choices":["judge","judged","judging","judges"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 8","choices":["asked","asks","ask","are asking"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 9","choices":["bringing","bring","brings","brought"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 10","choices":["develop","develops","developing","developed"],"answer":0}],"instructions":"Read the text. For each gap (1–10), choose the correct option (A, B, C or D).","curriculumFocus":"Identity"},{"id":"g9-A-R1","skill":"reading","group":1,"title":"Reading 1 — Matching","marks":4,"passage":"Match each person (1–4) with a suitable activity (A–E). One activity is extra.","people":["Hamad cycles to school most mornings, but the front light on his bicycle stopped working last week. He wants to understand the problem and repair the light himself rather than buy another bicycle.","Rana enjoys listening to her grandparents tell stories about family celebrations. She would like to interview older relatives, record their memories and make a collection that younger family members can enjoy.","Bilal volunteers with younger pupils after school. He notices that some children are nervous about reading aloud and wants to support them patiently while helping them discover books they find interesting.","Maryam lives in a flat without a garden. She hopes to grow herbs and flowers on a small balcony, but she needs advice about containers, sunlight and the right amount of water."],"options":["This reading club welcomes patient teenagers who want to help younger children. Members choose enjoyable books, listen as pupils practise reading and encourage them to talk about their favourite characters.","At the rooftop garden workshop, visitors learn how to grow vegetables and flowers in pots. A gardener demonstrates how to prepare soil, choose suitable plants and water them without wasting water.","The community repair hub offers practical demonstrations on looking after bicycles. Volunteers explain how lights and brakes work and help beginners carry out small repairs while following safety rules.","Members of this recording club use microphones to interview family members and neighbours. They learn how to ask respectful questions, save their audio files and organise personal stories into an archive.","At this sports centre, teams practise football and basketball with qualified coaches. The club welcomes confident athletes who want to improve fitness and prepare for competitions on Friday mornings."],"items":[{"stem":"Person 1: Hamad cycles to school most mornings, but the front light on his bicycle stopped working last week. He wants to understand the problem and repair the light himself rather than buy another bicycle.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Person 2: Rana enjoys listening to her grandparents tell stories about family celebrations. She would like to interview older relatives, record their memories and make a collection that younger family members can enjoy.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Person 3: Bilal volunteers with younger pupils after school. He notices that some children are nervous about reading aloud and wants to support them patiently while helping them discover books they find interesting.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Person 4: Maryam lives in a flat without a garden. She hopes to grow herbs and flowers on a small balcony, but she needs advice about containers, sunlight and the right amount of water.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":1}],"instructions":"Read the four short descriptions and the five activity descriptions. Match each person to an activity."},{"id":"g9-A-R2","skill":"reading","group":2,"title":"Reading 2 — Short answers","marks":6,"passage":"The town library recently began lending useful objects as well as books. The idea came from volunteers who noticed that families sometimes purchased equipment for a single project and never used it again. The new service offers tools for repairing furniture, simple measuring devices, and small kits for science activities. Users can borrow an item for one week after attending a short safety introduction. Some residents welcomed the scheme because it reduces unnecessary spending and encourages people to reuse resources. Others worried that borrowed equipment might be damaged or returned late. To address these concerns, the library introduced a booking system, clear instructions, and a team that checks every item when it comes back.\n\nAfter three months, the organisers collected feedback from sixty families. Most said they had completed a task they would otherwise have delayed. Several parents also reported working more closely with their children when using the science kits. Yet the project was not perfect. Popular tools were often unavailable during weekends, and some borrowers found the collection hours inconvenient. The library responded by extending opening hours on one afternoon and accepting suggestions for future equipment.\n\nThe coordinator believes that a library should offer more than objects to borrow. She wants residents to share practical knowledge, ask questions, and help one another. Next year, volunteers hope to provide repair demonstrations led by local experts. The council has not promised additional funding, so expansion will depend on donations and community involvement. The project illustrates how a small change to a familiar public service can encourage cooperation, but it also shows why organisers must listen carefully to users before deciding how to grow.","passageTitle":"A Library of Things","items":[{"stem":"Why did the library introduce the new service?","answers":["families bought equipment for one use"]},{"stem":"How long may people borrow an item?","answers":["one week"]},{"stem":"What do users attend before borrowing?","answers":["a short safety introduction"]},{"stem":"What problem affected weekends?","answers":["popular tools were unavailable"]},{"stem":"How did the library respond to inconvenient collection hours?","answers":["extended opening hours"]},{"stem":"What will determine whether the project expands?","answers":["donations and community involvement"]}],"instructions":"Read the text, then answer the six questions in short answers."},{"id":"g9-A-W1","skill":"writing","group":1,"title":"Writing 1 — Story","marks":5,"prompt":"Write a story about a small mistake that unexpectedly helped you learn something important. Describe the setting, the problem, and how it was resolved.","minWords":100},{"id":"g9-A-W2","skill":"writing","group":2,"title":"Writing 2 — Email","marks":5,"prompt":"Write an email to a friend explaining a creative community project you joined, what you did there, and why you would recommend it.","minWords":100}],"unit_id":1,"unit_name":"Identity","allocation":"unit_target","verification":"pending_curriculum_content_review","original_theme":"Practical community skills"},{"model":"B","grade":9,"minutes":120,"marks":40,"theme":"People and local heritage","tasks":[{"id":"g9-B-L1","skill":"listening","group":1,"title":"Listening 1 — Choose the picture","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"Which item did the speaker choose?","choices":["backpack","jacket","shoes"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-1-1.png","images/illustration-extra-1-2.png","images/illustration-extra-1-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"Text one. My cousins and I are preparing for a long walk around our neighbourhood. I thought about wearing my school jacket, but the afternoon will be warm. My old backpack is still strong. Instead, I bought comfortable shoes because the route includes several steep streets and narrow paths."},{"stem":"Which sport did the speaker join?","choices":["swimming","running","cycling"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-2-1.png","images/illustration-extra-2-2.png","images/illustration-extra-2-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"Text two. Last term I tried running with my brother and cycling with my friends. I enjoyed both, but I wanted to learn something different. When the new sports centre opened an indoor pool, I joined the swimming club. Now I practise twice a week and feel much more confident in the water."},{"stem":"Where will the public event take place?","choices":["stadium","school building","park"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-3-1.png","images/illustration-extra-3-2.png","images/illustration-extra-3-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"Text three. The youth festival needs a place for its final match. The school building is too small, and the public park has no seats for spectators. The organisers have booked the stadium instead. The match will begin there on Saturday afternoon, and families can enter through the main gate."},{"stem":"What weather does the latest forecast predict?","choices":["sunny","rainy","snowy"],"images":["images/illustration-3-1.png","images/illustration-3-2.png","images/illustration-3-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"Text four. Yesterday the weather website predicted rain, and my sister even worried about snow in the mountains. This morning the forecast changed. According to the latest update, the sky will be clear and sunny throughout the day. Our class can hold its outdoor activities as planned, but we should bring water."},{"stem":"Where will the club meet?","choices":["school","library","sports hall"],"images":["images/illustration-1-1.png","images/illustration-1-2.png","images/illustration-1-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"Text five. Our school photography club cannot use the sports hall because it is booked for a tournament. We considered the public library, but we need to display our pictures near the entrance. Our head teacher has now offered us a room at school. That is where we will meet next Thursday."}],"audio":"audio/g9-b-listening-1.mp3","transcript":"Text one. My cousins and I are preparing for a long walk around our neighbourhood. I thought about wearing my school jacket, but the afternoon will be warm. My old backpack is still strong. Instead, I bought comfortable shoes because the route includes several steep streets and narrow paths.\n\nText two. Last term I tried running with my brother and cycling with my friends. I enjoyed both, but I wanted to learn something different. When the new sports centre opened an indoor pool, I joined the swimming club. Now I practise twice a week and feel much more confident in the water.\n\nText three. The youth festival needs a place for its final match. The school building is too small, and the public park has no seats for spectators. The organisers have booked the stadium instead. The match will begin there on Saturday afternoon, and families can enter through the main gate.\n\nText four. Yesterday the weather website predicted rain, and my sister even worried about snow in the mountains. This morning the forecast changed. According to the latest update, the sky will be clear and sunny throughout the day. Our class can hold its outdoor activities as planned, but we should bring water.\n\nText five. Our school photography club cannot use the sports hall because it is booked for a tournament. We considered the public library, but we need to display our pictures near the entrance. Our head teacher has now offered us a room at school. That is where we will meet next Thursday.","instructions":"Listen to five short texts. For each text, choose the correct picture (A, B or C).","naturalAudioAvailable":true},{"id":"g9-B-L2","skill":"listening","group":2,"title":"Listening 2 — Interview","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"What did their first map show?","choices":["historic doors","local beaches","mountain animals"],"answer":0},{"stem":"When was the project launched?","choices":["November","February","June"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Who shared old photographs?","choices":["families","shopkeepers only","football coaches"],"answer":0},{"stem":"When does the team meet?","choices":["Monday morning","Thursday afternoon","Saturday evening"],"answer":1},{"stem":"What did the team learn?","choices":["making jewellery","checking photo sources","repairing cars"],"answer":1}],"audio":"audio/g9-b-listening-2.mp3","transcript":"Interviewer: Welcome to Young Historians. Today Salim is joining us to explain his community heritage-mapping project. Salim, what did your team put on its first map? Salim: We chose the historic doors in our neighbourhood. Some have unusual wooden patterns, so we walked along the old streets and carefully recorded their locations. Interviewer: That sounds interesting. When did your project begin? Salim: We launched it in November, just after a local museum invited schools to share ideas for protecting our heritage. Interviewer: Where did the old photographs come from? Salim: Families shared them with us. Parents and grandparents brought photographs of streets and houses that have changed over the years. Interviewer: How often do you work on the project? Salim: Our group meets on Thursday afternoons. That gives everyone time to finish lessons before we compare notes and plan our next visit. Interviewer: What important lesson have you learned? Salim: We must check the source of every photograph before publishing it. A date or place written on the back can help us avoid mistakes. Interviewer: And what happens next? Salim: We hope to add recorded memories from older residents. Their stories will explain why these places matter to the community. Interviewer: Thank you, Salim. Your project shows how history can connect generations.","instructions":"Listen to the interview twice. Choose the correct answer (A, B or C).","naturalAudioAvailable":true},{"id":"g9-B-GV","skill":"grammar_vocabulary","group":1,"title":"Grammar & Vocabulary — One cloze","marks":10,"passage":"Last month, our class (1) ____ a history project. While we (2) ____ old photographs, one student (3) ____ a story about the town market. Her grandfather (4) ____ there when he was young. We (5) ____ him to describe his memories, and he agreed. The team (6) ____ the interview when the school bell rang. Afterwards, we (7) ____ the recording and (8) ____ the dates. By the end of the week, our project (9) ____ generations together. We learned that shared memories can (10) ____ strong community connections.","items":[{"stem":"Gap 1","choices":["starting","starts","started","start"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 2","choices":["examines","were examining","examine","have examined"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 3","choices":["discovered","discovers","discovering","discover"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 4","choices":["works","working","work","worked"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 5","choices":["asking","asks","asked","ask"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 6","choices":["recorded","was recording","record","records"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 7","choices":["edited","editing","edit","edits"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 8","choices":["checking","checks","check","checked"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 9","choices":["bring","bringing","brought","brings"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 10","choices":["builds","build","built","building"],"answer":1}],"instructions":"Read the text. For each gap (1–10), choose the correct option (A, B, C or D).","curriculumFocus":"Connections"},{"id":"g9-B-R1","skill":"reading","group":1,"title":"Reading 1 — Matching","marks":4,"passage":"Match each person (1–4) with a suitable activity (A–E). One activity is extra.","people":["Saeed often reads news on social media, but he has noticed that some stories give misleading information. He wants to learn how to identify trustworthy sources before forwarding messages to his friends.","Amal likes designing useful objects rather than throwing materials away. She keeps cardboard, fabric and plastic containers at home and hopes to turn them into creative products with practical purposes.","Yusuf has interesting ideas, but he becomes nervous when speaking in front of a group. He wants a friendly environment where he can practise short presentations and become a more confident communicator.","Noura enjoys looking at the night sky with her family. She wants to understand the movement of the planets and keep a record of what she can observe during different months of the year."],"options":["In these public-speaking circles, students prepare short talks, practise using clear voices and receive supportive feedback. The atmosphere is relaxed, so even shy participants can develop confidence through regular practice.","Members of the astronomy society observe stars and planets from a safe outdoor location. They record changes in the sky, use simple maps and compare observations with information from science books.","The media literacy workshop teaches students to check photographs and compare information from different sources. Participants investigate misleading headlines and learn when they should question a story they find online.","At the reuse design studio, members collect unwanted objects and give them a new purpose. They plan products, select suitable materials and explain how creative recycling can protect the environment.","The beginners' swimming programme includes water-safety demonstrations and short supervised lessons in an indoor pool. Participants work on basic swimming movements and learn how to stay safe near water."],"items":[{"stem":"Person 1: Saeed often reads news on social media, but he has noticed that some stories give misleading information. He wants to learn how to identify trustworthy sources before forwarding messages to his friends.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Person 2: Amal likes designing useful objects rather than throwing materials away. She keeps cardboard, fabric and plastic containers at home and hopes to turn them into creative products with practical purposes.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Person 3: Yusuf has interesting ideas, but he becomes nervous when speaking in front of a group. He wants a friendly environment where he can practise short presentations and become a more confident communicator.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Person 4: Noura enjoys looking at the night sky with her family. She wants to understand the movement of the planets and keep a record of what she can observe during different months of the year.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":1}],"instructions":"Read the four short descriptions and the five activity descriptions. Match each person to an activity."},{"id":"g9-B-R2","skill":"reading","group":2,"title":"Reading 2 — Short answers","marks":6,"passage":"When a neighbourhood planned to improve its small square, the council invited teenagers to contribute ideas. Some adults were surprised because young people are rarely asked to comment on local planning. A group of pupils visited the site at different times of day. They recorded where people walked, which benches were used, and where shade was missing. Their first suggestion was an expensive new playground, but the council explained that the budget could not cover it.\n\nThe students then interviewed shopkeepers, parents, and older residents. Several adults preferred more seating, while children wanted room to play safely. The pupils revised their design, adding a shaded reading corner and keeping an open space for community events. A local architect helped them estimate costs, and they presented their work at a public meeting. Many residents praised the revised plan for considering people of different ages. However, others questioned whether teenagers understood the long-term costs of maintaining the site.\n\nThe council decided to test the plan using temporary benches and removable shade structures for two months. Volunteers collected comments during the trial, including reports about accessibility and cleanliness. The trial showed that the reading corner was popular, but its location blocked a busy pathway. The students moved it to a quieter area and prepared a second proposal.\n\nIn my view, young people should help design public spaces because they use them and notice problems that adults may overlook. Their ideas should not automatically become final decisions, though. Collaboration with engineers, local residents, and decision-makers helps turn enthusiasm into realistic improvements. The project was valuable because students learned to change their plans when new evidence appeared.","passageTitle":"Should Students Help Design Public Spaces?","items":[{"stem":"Who invited teenagers to suggest changes?","answers":["the council"]},{"stem":"What did pupils first suggest?","answers":["an expensive new playground"]},{"stem":"Why was the first proposal rejected?","answers":["the budget could not cover it"]},{"stem":"Who helped pupils estimate costs?","answers":["a local architect"]},{"stem":"How long did the council test the plan?","answers":["two months"]},{"stem":"Why does the writer support student involvement?","answers":["young people notice overlooked problems"]}],"instructions":"Read the text, then answer the six questions in short answers."},{"id":"g9-B-W1","skill":"writing","group":1,"title":"Writing 1 — Story","marks":5,"prompt":"Write a story beginning with: “The message arrived just as we were leaving.” Explain the unexpected event and its outcome.","minWords":100},{"id":"g9-B-W2","skill":"writing","group":2,"title":"Writing 2 — Email","marks":5,"prompt":"Write an email to a cousin about a useful skill you learned recently. Say how you learned it and how it could help in the future.","minWords":100}],"unit_id":2,"unit_name":"Connections","allocation":"unit_target","verification":"pending_curriculum_content_review","original_theme":"People and local heritage"},{"model":"C","grade":9,"minutes":120,"marks":40,"theme":"Wellbeing and responsible choices","tasks":[{"id":"g9-C-L1","skill":"listening","group":1,"title":"Listening 1 — Choose the picture","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"What is the family's weekend plan?","choices":["beach","camping","shopping"],"images":["images/illustration-5-1.png","images/illustration-5-2.png","images/illustration-5-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"My family considered different plans for the weekend on Monday. We considered beach and camping, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose shopping. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"What is the confirmed time of the event?","choices":["3 oclock","7 oclock","5 oclock"],"images":["images/illustration-4-1.png","images/illustration-4-2.png","images/illustration-4-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"The organisers discussed the starting time of the event on Monday. Some suggested 3 o’clock, while others preferred 5 o’clock. The official notice confirms 7 o’clock. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare. The"},{"stem":"Which item did the speaker choose?","choices":["backpack","jacket","shoes"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-1-1.png","images/illustration-extra-1-2.png","images/illustration-extra-1-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"I needed one useful item for my school activities on Monday. We considered backpack and shoes, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose jacket. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"Which hobby did the speaker choose?","choices":["art palette","football","camera"],"images":["images/illustration-2-1.png","images/illustration-2-2.png","images/illustration-2-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"I talked with my friends about a new hobby on Monday. We considered art palette and camera, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose football. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"Where will the public event take place?","choices":["stadium","school building","park"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-3-1.png","images/illustration-extra-3-2.png","images/illustration-extra-3-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"The organisers examined several locations for the public event on Monday. We considered stadium and park, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose school building. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."}],"audio":"audio/g9-c-listening-1.mp3","transcript":"Text 1. My family considered different plans for the weekend on Monday. We considered beach and camping, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose shopping. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 2. The organisers discussed the starting time of the event on Monday. Some suggested 3 o’clock, while others preferred 5 o’clock. The official notice confirms 7 o’clock. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare. The\n\nText 3. I needed one useful item for my school activities on Monday. We considered backpack and shoes, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose jacket. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 4. I talked with my friends about a new hobby on Monday. We considered art palette and camera, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose football. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 5. The organisers examined several locations for the public event on Monday. We considered stadium and park, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose school building. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.","instructions":"Listen to five short texts. For each text, choose the correct picture (A, B or C)."},{"id":"g9-C-L2","skill":"listening","group":2,"title":"Listening 2 — Interview","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"What was their first activity?","choices":["a football tournament","a quiet reading corner","a computer competition"],"answer":1},{"stem":"When did the group begin?","choices":["December","September","March"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Where do members come from?","choices":["Grade Five only","several Grade Nine classes","one teacher’s family"],"answer":1},{"stem":"When do they meet?","choices":["Sunday night","Friday afternoon","Wednesday lunch"],"answer":2},{"stem":"What have members learned?","choices":["building robots","cooking rice","respectful listening"],"answer":2}],"audio":"audio/g9-c-listening-2.mp3","transcript":"Interviewer: Welcome to our student voices programme. Today we are speaking with Alya about the student wellbeing group. Why was this project started? Alya: We wanted to help our school in a practical way, not just read about problems. Students suggested ideas, and our teachers helped us organise a small team. Interviewer: Tell us about your first activity. Alya: Their first activity was a quiet reading corner. We chose something manageable and listened to feedback from other students. Interviewer: When did everything begin? Alya: The group was established in September. We prepared the materials together and agreed on simple responsibilities. Interviewer: Who can get involved? Alya: Members come from several classes in Grade Nine. Everyone needs to show respect and take turns. Interviewer: When does the group meet? Alya: They meet during lunch on Wednesdays. We try to finish each session with a useful plan for the next one. Interviewer: What is the most important lesson? Alya: They have learned to listen without interrupting. It has also helped us become more confident when explaining our ideas. Interviewer: Would you recommend joining? Alya: Yes. Try something small, listen to others, and keep improving. Thank you for inviting me. We are proud that young people can lead meaningful changes, even when resources are limited and progress takes time.","instructions":"Listen to the interview twice. Choose the correct answer (A, B or C)."},{"id":"g9-C-GV","skill":"grammar_vocabulary","group":1,"title":"Grammar & Vocabulary — One cloze","marks":10,"passage":"Next year, our school (1) ____ a technology club. Students (2) ____ robots to help save water in the garden. One team is designing a (3) ____ that detects dry soil. The teacher thinks the invention (4) ____ useful. Before the exhibition, students (5) ____ test their designs. If a machine (6) ____ correctly, they will repair it. They (7) ____ need expensive equipment because many materials can be reused. We will use (8) ____ energy, and inventors (9) ____ make a difference. Everyone should (10) ____ new ideas responsibly.","items":[{"stem":"Gap 1","choices":["opened","opens yesterday","opening","will open"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 2","choices":["build yesterday","building now always","are going to build","built last year"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 3","choices":["suitcase","sensor","costume","invitation"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 4","choices":["will be","was","were","being"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 5","choices":["must to","mustn't to","should to","must"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 6","choices":["didn't working","not works","doesn't work","don't work"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 7","choices":["haven't","won't","didn't","wasn't"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 8","choices":["renewable","crowded","historic","noisy"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 9","choices":["can to","must to","can be to","can"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 10","choices":["exploring to","explores to","explore","explored yesterday"],"answer":2}],"instructions":"Read the text. For each gap (1–10), choose the correct option (A, B, C or D).","curriculumFocus":"The Future Is Now"},{"id":"g9-C-R1","skill":"reading","group":1,"title":"Reading 1 — Matching","marks":4,"passage":"Match each person (1–4) with a suitable activity (A–E). One activity is extra.","people":["Fatma has noticed that her family's electricity bill increases during the hottest months. She is interested in practical technology that could help homes use less power without making daily life uncomfortable.","Omar enjoys drawing modern buildings and traditional Omani houses. He hopes to improve his understanding of shape, distance and perspective so that his sketches look more realistic and detailed.","Khalid loves solving difficult problems with his classmates. He would enjoy activities that require careful thinking, teamwork and different strategies rather than simply memorising facts from a textbook.","Sara is curious about how people lived before electricity and modern transport. She wants to hear memories from older residents and visit places that show how daily life has changed."],"options":["The local heritage circle explores traditional homes and interviews older residents. Members compare old photographs with the present day and learn how daily routines, buildings and transport have changed.","This energy-saving team tests simple ways to reduce electricity use in homes and schools. Volunteers measure power consumption, explain efficient lighting and suggest changes that families can try.","In the puzzle laboratory, small groups work together on logic challenges and games. Members discuss possible solutions, test different approaches and learn that a difficult problem may have more than one method.","The sketching workshop teaches students how to draw streets and buildings using perspective. Participants practise with pencil and paper, compare architectural features and improve their drawings through feedback.","At the community music room, learners explore rhythms using drums and simple instruments. The programme focuses on keeping time, performing together and preparing short musical pieces for local events."],"items":[{"stem":"Person 1: Fatma has noticed that her family's electricity bill increases during the hottest months. She is interested in practical technology that could help homes use less power without making daily life uncomfortable.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Person 2: Omar enjoys drawing modern buildings and traditional Omani houses. He hopes to improve his understanding of shape, distance and perspective so that his sketches look more realistic and detailed.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Person 3: Khalid loves solving difficult problems with his classmates. He would enjoy activities that require careful thinking, teamwork and different strategies rather than simply memorising facts from a textbook.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Person 4: Sara is curious about how people lived before electricity and modern transport. She wants to hear memories from older residents and visit places that show how daily life has changed.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":0}],"instructions":"Read the four short descriptions and the five activity descriptions. Match each person to an activity."},{"id":"g9-C-R2","skill":"reading","group":2,"title":"Reading 2 — Short answers","marks":6,"passage":"A student environmental team wanted to understand why so many usable clothes were being discarded. They asked classmates to record the number of new items they bought during one month and explain what happened to older clothes. The results surprised them. Several students replaced perfectly good items simply because a different style was fashionable. Others donated clothes that no longer fitted, while a few repaired damaged items at home.\n\nThe team organised an exchange event in the school hall. Students could bring clean clothes in good condition and choose something another person had donated. Volunteers checked items carefully and sorted them by size. The event was free, but some parents worried that exchanging clothes might embarrass students who could not afford new ones. Organisers responded by avoiding labels about who had donated each item and presenting the event as a creative way to reduce waste for everyone.\n\nBy the end of the week, more than eighty garments had found new owners. The organisers also ran a short workshop showing how to repair loose buttons and small tears. One participant said she felt more confident after repairing her school bag without buying a replacement. Nevertheless, the team recognised that clothing exchange could not solve every environmental problem. Producing textiles still requires water and energy, and some items are difficult to reuse.\n\nThe head teacher argued that the most important lesson was not to stop buying clothes completely, but to make thoughtful decisions. Students should consider quality, need, repair, and donation before throwing things away. The project encouraged discussion rather than telling families what they must purchase. It showed that practical steps can help, especially when everyone is treated with respect.","passageTitle":"The Problem with Throwaway Fashion","items":[{"stem":"What were students asked to record?","answers":["new clothes bought in one month"]},{"stem":"Where was the exchange organised?","answers":["the school hall"]},{"stem":"Why did some parents worry?","answers":["students might feel embarrassed"]},{"stem":"How many garments found new owners?","answers":["more than eighty"]},{"stem":"What repair skill was demonstrated?","answers":["repairing loose buttons and tears"]},{"stem":"What does the head teacher recommend?","answers":["making thoughtful purchasing decisions"]}],"instructions":"Read the text, then answer the six questions in short answers."},{"id":"g9-C-W1","skill":"writing","group":1,"title":"Writing 1 — Story","marks":5,"prompt":"Write a story about a school event where a team had to change its plan at the last minute. Include a clear ending.","minWords":100},{"id":"g9-C-W2","skill":"writing","group":2,"title":"Writing 2 — Email","marks":5,"prompt":"Write an email to a friend suggesting an environmentally friendly activity for your school. Explain the steps and expected benefits.","minWords":100}],"unit_id":3,"unit_name":"The Future Is Now","allocation":"unit_target","verification":"pending_curriculum_content_review","original_theme":"Wellbeing and responsible choices"},{"model":"D","grade":9,"minutes":120,"marks":40,"theme":"Mixed practice across three draft banks","allocation":"mixed","unit_id":null,"verification":"mixed_structure_valid_unit_content_pending","source_models":["A","B","C","B","C","A","B"],"tasks":[{"id":"g9-A-L1","skill":"listening","group":1,"title":"Listening 1 — Choose the picture","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"Where will the club meet?","choices":["school","library","sports hall"],"images":["images/illustration-1-1.png","images/illustration-1-2.png","images/illustration-1-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"Our environment club is planning its next meeting. We usually meet at school, but the hall is being repaired. Someone suggested the sports centre, but it closes too early. This Thursday, we will use the public library, where a quiet room has already been booked."},{"stem":"Which hobby did the speaker choose?","choices":["art palette","football","camera"],"images":["images/illustration-2-1.png","images/illustration-2-2.png","images/illustration-2-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"I wanted to try something creative after school. My brother plays football, and my cousin takes photographs. I enjoy both, but this term I have joined an art club. I have bought a paintbrush and a small palette because we are learning watercolour painting."},{"stem":"What weather does the latest forecast predict?","choices":["sunny","rainy","snowy"],"images":["images/illustration-3-1.png","images/illustration-3-2.png","images/illustration-3-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"We are planning a class trip tomorrow, so everyone has checked the weather. Yesterday, the forecast showed sunshine, and one website even mentioned cold winds. This morning, however, the updated forecast says there will be rain for most of the day. Remember to bring a jacket."},{"stem":"What is the confirmed time of the event?","choices":["3 oclock","7 oclock","5 oclock"],"images":["images/illustration-4-1.png","images/illustration-4-2.png","images/illustration-4-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"Please note a change to the community festival programme. Some posters showed three o'clock, and an early email said seven o'clock. The organisers have now confirmed that the opening performance will begin at five o'clock. Please arrive at least fifteen minutes before the show."},{"stem":"What is the family's weekend plan?","choices":["beach","camping","shopping"],"images":["images/illustration-5-1.png","images/illustration-5-2.png","images/illustration-5-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"My family discussed what to do this weekend. My sister wanted to go shopping, and my father suggested spending the afternoon at the beach. We finally decided to go camping in the mountains, because everyone wanted to spend the night outdoors and watch the stars."}],"audio":"audio/g9-a-listening-1.mp3","transcript":"Text 1. Our environment club is planning its next meeting. We usually meet at school, but the hall is being repaired. Someone suggested the sports centre, but it closes too early. This Thursday, we will use the public library, where a quiet room has already been booked.\n\nText 2. I wanted to try something creative after school. My brother plays football, and my cousin takes photographs. I enjoy both, but this term I have joined an art club. I have bought a paintbrush and a small palette because we are learning watercolour painting.\n\nText 3. We are planning a class trip tomorrow, so everyone has checked the weather. Yesterday, the forecast showed sunshine, and one website even mentioned cold winds. This morning, however, the updated forecast says there will be rain for most of the day. Remember to bring a jacket.\n\nText 4. Please note a change to the community festival programme. Some posters showed three o'clock, and an early email said seven o'clock. The organisers have now confirmed that the opening performance will begin at five o'clock. Please arrive at least fifteen minutes before the show.\n\nText 5. My family discussed what to do this weekend. My sister wanted to go shopping, and my father suggested spending the afternoon at the beach. We finally decided to go camping in the mountains, because everyone wanted to spend the night outdoors and watch the stars.","instructions":"Listen to five short texts. For each text, choose the correct picture (A, B or C).","naturalAudioAvailable":true,"source_model":"A"},{"id":"g9-B-L2","skill":"listening","group":2,"title":"Listening 2 — Interview","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"What did their first map show?","choices":["historic doors","local beaches","mountain animals"],"answer":0},{"stem":"When was the project launched?","choices":["November","February","June"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Who shared old photographs?","choices":["families","shopkeepers only","football coaches"],"answer":0},{"stem":"When does the team meet?","choices":["Monday morning","Thursday afternoon","Saturday evening"],"answer":1},{"stem":"What did the team learn?","choices":["making jewellery","checking photo sources","repairing cars"],"answer":1}],"audio":"audio/g9-b-listening-2.mp3","transcript":"Interviewer: Welcome to Young Historians. Today Salim is joining us to explain his community heritage-mapping project. Salim, what did your team put on its first map? Salim: We chose the historic doors in our neighbourhood. Some have unusual wooden patterns, so we walked along the old streets and carefully recorded their locations. Interviewer: That sounds interesting. When did your project begin? Salim: We launched it in November, just after a local museum invited schools to share ideas for protecting our heritage. Interviewer: Where did the old photographs come from? Salim: Families shared them with us. Parents and grandparents brought photographs of streets and houses that have changed over the years. Interviewer: How often do you work on the project? Salim: Our group meets on Thursday afternoons. That gives everyone time to finish lessons before we compare notes and plan our next visit. Interviewer: What important lesson have you learned? Salim: We must check the source of every photograph before publishing it. A date or place written on the back can help us avoid mistakes. Interviewer: And what happens next? Salim: We hope to add recorded memories from older residents. Their stories will explain why these places matter to the community. Interviewer: Thank you, Salim. Your project shows how history can connect generations.","instructions":"Listen to the interview twice. Choose the correct answer (A, B or C).","source_model":"B","naturalAudioAvailable":true},{"id":"g9-C-GV","skill":"grammar_vocabulary","group":1,"title":"Grammar & Vocabulary — One cloze","marks":10,"passage":"Next year, our school (1) ____ a technology club. Students (2) ____ robots to help save water in the garden. One team is designing a (3) ____ that detects dry soil. The teacher thinks the invention (4) ____ useful. Before the exhibition, students (5) ____ test their designs. If a machine (6) ____ correctly, they will repair it. They (7) ____ need expensive equipment because many materials can be reused. We will use (8) ____ energy, and inventors (9) ____ make a difference. Everyone should (10) ____ new ideas responsibly.","items":[{"stem":"Gap 1","choices":["opened","opens yesterday","opening","will open"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 2","choices":["build yesterday","building now always","are going to build","built last year"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 3","choices":["suitcase","sensor","costume","invitation"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 4","choices":["will be","was","were","being"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 5","choices":["must to","mustn't to","should to","must"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 6","choices":["didn't working","not works","doesn't work","don't work"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 7","choices":["haven't","won't","didn't","wasn't"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 8","choices":["renewable","crowded","historic","noisy"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 9","choices":["can to","must to","can be to","can"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 10","choices":["exploring to","explores to","explore","explored yesterday"],"answer":2}],"instructions":"Read the text. For each gap (1–10), choose the correct option (A, B, C or D).","source_model":"C","curriculumFocus":"The Future Is Now"},{"id":"g9-B-R1","skill":"reading","group":1,"title":"Reading 1 — Matching","marks":4,"passage":"Match each person (1–4) with a suitable activity (A–E). One activity is extra.","people":["Saeed often reads news on social media, but he has noticed that some stories give misleading information. He wants to learn how to identify trustworthy sources before forwarding messages to his friends.","Amal likes designing useful objects rather than throwing materials away. She keeps cardboard, fabric and plastic containers at home and hopes to turn them into creative products with practical purposes.","Yusuf has interesting ideas, but he becomes nervous when speaking in front of a group. He wants a friendly environment where he can practise short presentations and become a more confident communicator.","Noura enjoys looking at the night sky with her family. She wants to understand the movement of the planets and keep a record of what she can observe during different months of the year."],"options":["In these public-speaking circles, students prepare short talks, practise using clear voices and receive supportive feedback. The atmosphere is relaxed, so even shy participants can develop confidence through regular practice.","Members of the astronomy society observe stars and planets from a safe outdoor location. They record changes in the sky, use simple maps and compare observations with information from science books.","The media literacy workshop teaches students to check photographs and compare information from different sources. Participants investigate misleading headlines and learn when they should question a story they find online.","At the reuse design studio, members collect unwanted objects and give them a new purpose. They plan products, select suitable materials and explain how creative recycling can protect the environment.","The beginners' swimming programme includes water-safety demonstrations and short supervised lessons in an indoor pool. Participants work on basic swimming movements and learn how to stay safe near water."],"items":[{"stem":"Person 1: Saeed often reads news on social media, but he has noticed that some stories give misleading information. He wants to learn how to identify trustworthy sources before forwarding messages to his friends.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Person 2: Amal likes designing useful objects rather than throwing materials away. She keeps cardboard, fabric and plastic containers at home and hopes to turn them into creative products with practical purposes.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Person 3: Yusuf has interesting ideas, but he becomes nervous when speaking in front of a group. He wants a friendly environment where he can practise short presentations and become a more confident communicator.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Person 4: Noura enjoys looking at the night sky with her family. She wants to understand the movement of the planets and keep a record of what she can observe during different months of the year.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":1}],"instructions":"Read the four short descriptions and the five activity descriptions. Match each person to an activity.","source_model":"B"},{"id":"g9-C-R2","skill":"reading","group":2,"title":"Reading 2 — Short answers","marks":6,"passage":"A student environmental team wanted to understand why so many usable clothes were being discarded. They asked classmates to record the number of new items they bought during one month and explain what happened to older clothes. The results surprised them. Several students replaced perfectly good items simply because a different style was fashionable. Others donated clothes that no longer fitted, while a few repaired damaged items at home.\n\nThe team organised an exchange event in the school hall. Students could bring clean clothes in good condition and choose something another person had donated. Volunteers checked items carefully and sorted them by size. The event was free, but some parents worried that exchanging clothes might embarrass students who could not afford new ones. Organisers responded by avoiding labels about who had donated each item and presenting the event as a creative way to reduce waste for everyone.\n\nBy the end of the week, more than eighty garments had found new owners. The organisers also ran a short workshop showing how to repair loose buttons and small tears. One participant said she felt more confident after repairing her school bag without buying a replacement. Nevertheless, the team recognised that clothing exchange could not solve every environmental problem. Producing textiles still requires water and energy, and some items are difficult to reuse.\n\nThe head teacher argued that the most important lesson was not to stop buying clothes completely, but to make thoughtful decisions. Students should consider quality, need, repair, and donation before throwing things away. The project encouraged discussion rather than telling families what they must purchase. It showed that practical steps can help, especially when everyone is treated with respect.","passageTitle":"The Problem with Throwaway Fashion","items":[{"stem":"What were students asked to record?","answers":["new clothes bought in one month"]},{"stem":"Where was the exchange organised?","answers":["the school hall"]},{"stem":"Why did some parents worry?","answers":["students might feel embarrassed"]},{"stem":"How many garments found new owners?","answers":["more than eighty"]},{"stem":"What repair skill was demonstrated?","answers":["repairing loose buttons and tears"]},{"stem":"What does the head teacher recommend?","answers":["making thoughtful purchasing decisions"]}],"instructions":"Read the text, then answer the six questions in short answers.","source_model":"C"},{"id":"g9-A-W1","skill":"writing","group":1,"title":"Writing 1 — Story","marks":5,"prompt":"Write a story about a small mistake that unexpectedly helped you learn something important. Describe the setting, the problem, and how it was resolved.","minWords":100,"source_model":"A"},{"id":"g9-B-W2","skill":"writing","group":2,"title":"Writing 2 — Email","marks":5,"prompt":"Write an email to a cousin about a useful skill you learned recently. Say how you learned it and how it could help in the future.","minWords":100,"source_model":"B"}]},{"model":"E","grade":9,"minutes":120,"marks":40,"theme":"Mixed practice across three draft banks","allocation":"mixed","unit_id":null,"verification":"mixed_structure_valid_unit_content_pending","source_models":["B","C","A","C","A","B","C"],"tasks":[{"id":"g9-B-L1","skill":"listening","group":1,"title":"Listening 1 — Choose the picture","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"Which item did the speaker choose?","choices":["backpack","jacket","shoes"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-1-1.png","images/illustration-extra-1-2.png","images/illustration-extra-1-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"Text one. My cousins and I are preparing for a long walk around our neighbourhood. I thought about wearing my school jacket, but the afternoon will be warm. My old backpack is still strong. Instead, I bought comfortable shoes because the route includes several steep streets and narrow paths."},{"stem":"Which sport did the speaker join?","choices":["swimming","running","cycling"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-2-1.png","images/illustration-extra-2-2.png","images/illustration-extra-2-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"Text two. Last term I tried running with my brother and cycling with my friends. I enjoyed both, but I wanted to learn something different. When the new sports centre opened an indoor pool, I joined the swimming club. Now I practise twice a week and feel much more confident in the water."},{"stem":"Where will the public event take place?","choices":["stadium","school building","park"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-3-1.png","images/illustration-extra-3-2.png","images/illustration-extra-3-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"Text three. The youth festival needs a place for its final match. The school building is too small, and the public park has no seats for spectators. The organisers have booked the stadium instead. The match will begin there on Saturday afternoon, and families can enter through the main gate."},{"stem":"What weather does the latest forecast predict?","choices":["sunny","rainy","snowy"],"images":["images/illustration-3-1.png","images/illustration-3-2.png","images/illustration-3-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"Text four. Yesterday the weather website predicted rain, and my sister even worried about snow in the mountains. This morning the forecast changed. According to the latest update, the sky will be clear and sunny throughout the day. Our class can hold its outdoor activities as planned, but we should bring water."},{"stem":"Where will the club meet?","choices":["school","library","sports hall"],"images":["images/illustration-1-1.png","images/illustration-1-2.png","images/illustration-1-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"Text five. Our school photography club cannot use the sports hall because it is booked for a tournament. We considered the public library, but we need to display our pictures near the entrance. Our head teacher has now offered us a room at school. That is where we will meet next Thursday."}],"audio":"audio/g9-b-listening-1.mp3","transcript":"Text one. My cousins and I are preparing for a long walk around our neighbourhood. I thought about wearing my school jacket, but the afternoon will be warm. My old backpack is still strong. Instead, I bought comfortable shoes because the route includes several steep streets and narrow paths.\n\nText two. Last term I tried running with my brother and cycling with my friends. I enjoyed both, but I wanted to learn something different. When the new sports centre opened an indoor pool, I joined the swimming club. Now I practise twice a week and feel much more confident in the water.\n\nText three. The youth festival needs a place for its final match. The school building is too small, and the public park has no seats for spectators. The organisers have booked the stadium instead. The match will begin there on Saturday afternoon, and families can enter through the main gate.\n\nText four. Yesterday the weather website predicted rain, and my sister even worried about snow in the mountains. This morning the forecast changed. According to the latest update, the sky will be clear and sunny throughout the day. Our class can hold its outdoor activities as planned, but we should bring water.\n\nText five. Our school photography club cannot use the sports hall because it is booked for a tournament. We considered the public library, but we need to display our pictures near the entrance. Our head teacher has now offered us a room at school. That is where we will meet next Thursday.","instructions":"Listen to five short texts. For each text, choose the correct picture (A, B or C).","source_model":"B","naturalAudioAvailable":true},{"id":"g9-C-L2","skill":"listening","group":2,"title":"Listening 2 — Interview","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"What was their first activity?","choices":["a football tournament","a quiet reading corner","a computer competition"],"answer":1},{"stem":"When did the group begin?","choices":["December","September","March"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Where do members come from?","choices":["Grade Five only","several Grade Nine classes","one teacher’s family"],"answer":1},{"stem":"When do they meet?","choices":["Sunday night","Friday afternoon","Wednesday lunch"],"answer":2},{"stem":"What have members learned?","choices":["building robots","cooking rice","respectful listening"],"answer":2}],"audio":"audio/g9-c-listening-2.mp3","transcript":"Interviewer: Welcome to our student voices programme. Today we are speaking with Alya about the student wellbeing group. Why was this project started? Alya: We wanted to help our school in a practical way, not just read about problems. Students suggested ideas, and our teachers helped us organise a small team. Interviewer: Tell us about your first activity. Alya: Their first activity was a quiet reading corner. We chose something manageable and listened to feedback from other students. Interviewer: When did everything begin? Alya: The group was established in September. We prepared the materials together and agreed on simple responsibilities. Interviewer: Who can get involved? Alya: Members come from several classes in Grade Nine. Everyone needs to show respect and take turns. Interviewer: When does the group meet? Alya: They meet during lunch on Wednesdays. We try to finish each session with a useful plan for the next one. Interviewer: What is the most important lesson? Alya: They have learned to listen without interrupting. It has also helped us become more confident when explaining our ideas. Interviewer: Would you recommend joining? Alya: Yes. Try something small, listen to others, and keep improving. Thank you for inviting me. We are proud that young people can lead meaningful changes, even when resources are limited and progress takes time.","instructions":"Listen to the interview twice. Choose the correct answer (A, B or C).","source_model":"C"},{"id":"g9-A-GV","skill":"grammar_vocabulary","group":1,"title":"Grammar & Vocabulary — One cloze","marks":10,"passage":"Everyone (1) ____ an identity. My friend Huda usually (2) ____ stories with her grandmother, but today she (3) ____ a video about her family. We (4) ____ proud of our languages and customs. Huda is very (5) ____ when she speaks to visitors. She (6) ____ her ideas and never (7) ____ anyone unfairly. At the moment, her classmates (8) ____ questions about the video. They want to know how traditions (9) ____ people together. Huda believes that everyone should (10) ____ their own interests while respecting others.","items":[{"stem":"Gap 1","choices":["had","has","have","having"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 2","choices":["shares","share","shared","sharing"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 3","choices":["is making","made","make","makes"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 4","choices":["was","be","are","is"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 5","choices":["confide","confident","confidence","confidently"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 6","choices":["explains","explain","explaining","explained"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 7","choices":["judge","judged","judging","judges"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 8","choices":["asked","asks","ask","are asking"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 9","choices":["bringing","bring","brings","brought"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 10","choices":["develop","develops","developing","developed"],"answer":0}],"instructions":"Read the text. For each gap (1–10), choose the correct option (A, B, C or D).","source_model":"A","curriculumFocus":"Identity"},{"id":"g9-C-R1","skill":"reading","group":1,"title":"Reading 1 — Matching","marks":4,"passage":"Match each person (1–4) with a suitable activity (A–E). One activity is extra.","people":["Fatma has noticed that her family's electricity bill increases during the hottest months. She is interested in practical technology that could help homes use less power without making daily life uncomfortable.","Omar enjoys drawing modern buildings and traditional Omani houses. He hopes to improve his understanding of shape, distance and perspective so that his sketches look more realistic and detailed.","Khalid loves solving difficult problems with his classmates. He would enjoy activities that require careful thinking, teamwork and different strategies rather than simply memorising facts from a textbook.","Sara is curious about how people lived before electricity and modern transport. She wants to hear memories from older residents and visit places that show how daily life has changed."],"options":["The local heritage circle explores traditional homes and interviews older residents. Members compare old photographs with the present day and learn how daily routines, buildings and transport have changed.","This energy-saving team tests simple ways to reduce electricity use in homes and schools. Volunteers measure power consumption, explain efficient lighting and suggest changes that families can try.","In the puzzle laboratory, small groups work together on logic challenges and games. Members discuss possible solutions, test different approaches and learn that a difficult problem may have more than one method.","The sketching workshop teaches students how to draw streets and buildings using perspective. Participants practise with pencil and paper, compare architectural features and improve their drawings through feedback.","At the community music room, learners explore rhythms using drums and simple instruments. The programme focuses on keeping time, performing together and preparing short musical pieces for local events."],"items":[{"stem":"Person 1: Fatma has noticed that her family's electricity bill increases during the hottest months. She is interested in practical technology that could help homes use less power without making daily life uncomfortable.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Person 2: Omar enjoys drawing modern buildings and traditional Omani houses. He hopes to improve his understanding of shape, distance and perspective so that his sketches look more realistic and detailed.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Person 3: Khalid loves solving difficult problems with his classmates. He would enjoy activities that require careful thinking, teamwork and different strategies rather than simply memorising facts from a textbook.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Person 4: Sara is curious about how people lived before electricity and modern transport. She wants to hear memories from older residents and visit places that show how daily life has changed.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":0}],"instructions":"Read the four short descriptions and the five activity descriptions. Match each person to an activity.","source_model":"C"},{"id":"g9-A-R2","skill":"reading","group":2,"title":"Reading 2 — Short answers","marks":6,"passage":"The town library recently began lending useful objects as well as books. The idea came from volunteers who noticed that families sometimes purchased equipment for a single project and never used it again. The new service offers tools for repairing furniture, simple measuring devices, and small kits for science activities. Users can borrow an item for one week after attending a short safety introduction. Some residents welcomed the scheme because it reduces unnecessary spending and encourages people to reuse resources. Others worried that borrowed equipment might be damaged or returned late. To address these concerns, the library introduced a booking system, clear instructions, and a team that checks every item when it comes back.\n\nAfter three months, the organisers collected feedback from sixty families. Most said they had completed a task they would otherwise have delayed. Several parents also reported working more closely with their children when using the science kits. Yet the project was not perfect. Popular tools were often unavailable during weekends, and some borrowers found the collection hours inconvenient. The library responded by extending opening hours on one afternoon and accepting suggestions for future equipment.\n\nThe coordinator believes that a library should offer more than objects to borrow. She wants residents to share practical knowledge, ask questions, and help one another. Next year, volunteers hope to provide repair demonstrations led by local experts. The council has not promised additional funding, so expansion will depend on donations and community involvement. The project illustrates how a small change to a familiar public service can encourage cooperation, but it also shows why organisers must listen carefully to users before deciding how to grow.","passageTitle":"A Library of Things","items":[{"stem":"Why did the library introduce the new service?","answers":["families bought equipment for one use"]},{"stem":"How long may people borrow an item?","answers":["one week"]},{"stem":"What do users attend before borrowing?","answers":["a short safety introduction"]},{"stem":"What problem affected weekends?","answers":["popular tools were unavailable"]},{"stem":"How did the library respond to inconvenient collection hours?","answers":["extended opening hours"]},{"stem":"What will determine whether the project expands?","answers":["donations and community involvement"]}],"instructions":"Read the text, then answer the six questions in short answers.","source_model":"A"},{"id":"g9-B-W1","skill":"writing","group":1,"title":"Writing 1 — Story","marks":5,"prompt":"Write a story beginning with: “The message arrived just as we were leaving.” Explain the unexpected event and its outcome.","minWords":100,"source_model":"B"},{"id":"g9-C-W2","skill":"writing","group":2,"title":"Writing 2 — Email","marks":5,"prompt":"Write an email to a friend suggesting an environmentally friendly activity for your school. Explain the steps and expected benefits.","minWords":100,"source_model":"C"}]},{"model":"F","grade":9,"minutes":120,"marks":40,"theme":"Mixed practice across three draft banks","allocation":"mixed","unit_id":null,"verification":"mixed_structure_valid_unit_content_pending","source_models":["C","A","B","A","B","C","A"],"tasks":[{"id":"g9-C-L1","skill":"listening","group":1,"title":"Listening 1 — Choose the picture","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"What is the family's weekend plan?","choices":["beach","camping","shopping"],"images":["images/illustration-5-1.png","images/illustration-5-2.png","images/illustration-5-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"My family considered different plans for the weekend on Monday. We considered beach and camping, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose shopping. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"What is the confirmed time of the event?","choices":["3 oclock","7 oclock","5 oclock"],"images":["images/illustration-4-1.png","images/illustration-4-2.png","images/illustration-4-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"The organisers discussed the starting time of the event on Monday. Some suggested 3 o’clock, while others preferred 5 o’clock. The official notice confirms 7 o’clock. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare. The"},{"stem":"Which item did the speaker choose?","choices":["backpack","jacket","shoes"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-1-1.png","images/illustration-extra-1-2.png","images/illustration-extra-1-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"I needed one useful item for my school activities on Monday. We considered backpack and shoes, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose jacket. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"Which hobby did the speaker choose?","choices":["art palette","football","camera"],"images":["images/illustration-2-1.png","images/illustration-2-2.png","images/illustration-2-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"I talked with my friends about a new hobby on Monday. We considered art palette and camera, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose football. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"Where will the public event take place?","choices":["stadium","school building","park"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-3-1.png","images/illustration-extra-3-2.png","images/illustration-extra-3-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"The organisers examined several locations for the public event on Monday. We considered stadium and park, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose school building. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."}],"audio":"audio/g9-c-listening-1.mp3","transcript":"Text 1. My family considered different plans for the weekend on Monday. We considered beach and camping, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose shopping. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 2. The organisers discussed the starting time of the event on Monday. Some suggested 3 o’clock, while others preferred 5 o’clock. The official notice confirms 7 o’clock. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare. The\n\nText 3. I needed one useful item for my school activities on Monday. We considered backpack and shoes, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose jacket. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 4. I talked with my friends about a new hobby on Monday. We considered art palette and camera, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose football. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 5. The organisers examined several locations for the public event on Monday. We considered stadium and park, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose school building. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.","instructions":"Listen to five short texts. For each text, choose the correct picture (A, B or C).","source_model":"C"},{"id":"g9-A-L2","skill":"listening","group":2,"title":"Listening 2 — Interview","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"Where was the first episode recorded?","choices":["the library","the sports hall","the old science classroom"],"answer":2},{"stem":"When did the team start?","choices":["September","January","October"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Who may apply?","choices":["teachers only","Grade Twelve only","Grades Eight and Nine"],"answer":2},{"stem":"When do they meet?","choices":["Tuesday after school","Friday morning","Sunday evening"],"answer":0},{"stem":"What skill did they learn?","choices":["checking facts","painting walls","playing football"],"answer":0}],"audio":"audio/g9-a-listening-2.mp3","transcript":"Interviewer: Welcome to Student Voices. Today we are speaking with Maha about the community radio project. Why was this project started?\n\nMaha: We wanted to help our school in a practical way, not just read about problems. Students suggested ideas, and our teachers helped us organise a small team.\n\nInterviewer: Tell us about your first activity.\n\nMaha: The first episode was recorded in the old science classroom. We chose something manageable and listened to feedback from other students.\n\nInterviewer: When did everything begin?\n\nMaha: The team began in October. We prepared the materials together and agreed on simple responsibilities.\n\nInterviewer: Who can get involved?\n\nMaha: Students from Grades Eight and Nine can apply. Everyone needs to show respect and take turns.\n\nInterviewer: When does the group meet?\n\nMaha: We meet every Tuesday after school. We try to finish each session with a useful plan for the next one.\n\nInterviewer: What is the most important lesson?\n\nMaha: We learned how to check a story before sharing it. It has also helped us become more confident when explaining our ideas.\n\nInterviewer: Would you recommend joining?\n\nMaha: Yes. Try something small, listen to others, and keep improving. We are proud that young people can lead meaningful changes, even when resources are limited and progress takes time. Thank you for inviting me.","instructions":"Listen to the interview twice. Choose the correct answer (A, B or C).","naturalAudioAvailable":true,"source_model":"A"},{"id":"g9-B-GV","skill":"grammar_vocabulary","group":1,"title":"Grammar & Vocabulary — One cloze","marks":10,"passage":"Last month, our class (1) ____ a history project. While we (2) ____ old photographs, one student (3) ____ a story about the town market. Her grandfather (4) ____ there when he was young. We (5) ____ him to describe his memories, and he agreed. The team (6) ____ the interview when the school bell rang. Afterwards, we (7) ____ the recording and (8) ____ the dates. By the end of the week, our project (9) ____ generations together. We learned that shared memories can (10) ____ strong community connections.","items":[{"stem":"Gap 1","choices":["starting","starts","started","start"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 2","choices":["examines","were examining","examine","have examined"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 3","choices":["discovered","discovers","discovering","discover"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 4","choices":["works","working","work","worked"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 5","choices":["asking","asks","asked","ask"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 6","choices":["recorded","was recording","record","records"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 7","choices":["edited","editing","edit","edits"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 8","choices":["checking","checks","check","checked"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 9","choices":["bring","bringing","brought","brings"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 10","choices":["builds","build","built","building"],"answer":1}],"instructions":"Read the text. For each gap (1–10), choose the correct option (A, B, C or D).","source_model":"B","curriculumFocus":"Connections"},{"id":"g9-A-R1","skill":"reading","group":1,"title":"Reading 1 — Matching","marks":4,"passage":"Match each person (1–4) with a suitable activity (A–E). One activity is extra.","people":["Hamad cycles to school most mornings, but the front light on his bicycle stopped working last week. He wants to understand the problem and repair the light himself rather than buy another bicycle.","Rana enjoys listening to her grandparents tell stories about family celebrations. She would like to interview older relatives, record their memories and make a collection that younger family members can enjoy.","Bilal volunteers with younger pupils after school. He notices that some children are nervous about reading aloud and wants to support them patiently while helping them discover books they find interesting.","Maryam lives in a flat without a garden. She hopes to grow herbs and flowers on a small balcony, but she needs advice about containers, sunlight and the right amount of water."],"options":["This reading club welcomes patient teenagers who want to help younger children. Members choose enjoyable books, listen as pupils practise reading and encourage them to talk about their favourite characters.","At the rooftop garden workshop, visitors learn how to grow vegetables and flowers in pots. A gardener demonstrates how to prepare soil, choose suitable plants and water them without wasting water.","The community repair hub offers practical demonstrations on looking after bicycles. Volunteers explain how lights and brakes work and help beginners carry out small repairs while following safety rules.","Members of this recording club use microphones to interview family members and neighbours. They learn how to ask respectful questions, save their audio files and organise personal stories into an archive.","At this sports centre, teams practise football and basketball with qualified coaches. The club welcomes confident athletes who want to improve fitness and prepare for competitions on Friday mornings."],"items":[{"stem":"Person 1: Hamad cycles to school most mornings, but the front light on his bicycle stopped working last week. He wants to understand the problem and repair the light himself rather than buy another bicycle.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Person 2: Rana enjoys listening to her grandparents tell stories about family celebrations. She would like to interview older relatives, record their memories and make a collection that younger family members can enjoy.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Person 3: Bilal volunteers with younger pupils after school. He notices that some children are nervous about reading aloud and wants to support them patiently while helping them discover books they find interesting.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Person 4: Maryam lives in a flat without a garden. She hopes to grow herbs and flowers on a small balcony, but she needs advice about containers, sunlight and the right amount of water.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":1}],"instructions":"Read the four short descriptions and the five activity descriptions. Match each person to an activity.","source_model":"A"},{"id":"g9-B-R2","skill":"reading","group":2,"title":"Reading 2 — Short answers","marks":6,"passage":"When a neighbourhood planned to improve its small square, the council invited teenagers to contribute ideas. Some adults were surprised because young people are rarely asked to comment on local planning. A group of pupils visited the site at different times of day. They recorded where people walked, which benches were used, and where shade was missing. Their first suggestion was an expensive new playground, but the council explained that the budget could not cover it.\n\nThe students then interviewed shopkeepers, parents, and older residents. Several adults preferred more seating, while children wanted room to play safely. The pupils revised their design, adding a shaded reading corner and keeping an open space for community events. A local architect helped them estimate costs, and they presented their work at a public meeting. Many residents praised the revised plan for considering people of different ages. However, others questioned whether teenagers understood the long-term costs of maintaining the site.\n\nThe council decided to test the plan using temporary benches and removable shade structures for two months. Volunteers collected comments during the trial, including reports about accessibility and cleanliness. The trial showed that the reading corner was popular, but its location blocked a busy pathway. The students moved it to a quieter area and prepared a second proposal.\n\nIn my view, young people should help design public spaces because they use them and notice problems that adults may overlook. Their ideas should not automatically become final decisions, though. Collaboration with engineers, local residents, and decision-makers helps turn enthusiasm into realistic improvements. The project was valuable because students learned to change their plans when new evidence appeared.","passageTitle":"Should Students Help Design Public Spaces?","items":[{"stem":"Who invited teenagers to suggest changes?","answers":["the council"]},{"stem":"What did pupils first suggest?","answers":["an expensive new playground"]},{"stem":"Why was the first proposal rejected?","answers":["the budget could not cover it"]},{"stem":"Who helped pupils estimate costs?","answers":["a local architect"]},{"stem":"How long did the council test the plan?","answers":["two months"]},{"stem":"Why does the writer support student involvement?","answers":["young people notice overlooked problems"]}],"instructions":"Read the text, then answer the six questions in short answers.","source_model":"B"},{"id":"g9-C-W1","skill":"writing","group":1,"title":"Writing 1 — Story","marks":5,"prompt":"Write a story about a school event where a team had to change its plan at the last minute. Include a clear ending.","minWords":100,"source_model":"C"},{"id":"g9-A-W2","skill":"writing","group":2,"title":"Writing 2 — Email","marks":5,"prompt":"Write an email to a friend explaining a creative community project you joined, what you did there, and why you would recommend it.","minWords":100,"source_model":"A"}]},{"model":"G","grade":9,"minutes":120,"marks":40,"theme":"Inventive ideas and local solutions","tasks":[{"id":"g9-D-L1","skill":"listening","group":1,"title":"Listening 1 — Choose the picture","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"Which sport did the speaker join?","choices":["swimming","running","cycling"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-2-1.png","images/illustration-extra-2-2.png","images/illustration-extra-2-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"I compared different sports before joining a school club during our planning meeting. We considered swimming and running, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose cycling. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"Where will the public event take place?","choices":["stadium","school building","park"],"images":["images/illustration-extra-3-1.png","images/illustration-extra-3-2.png","images/illustration-extra-3-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"The organisers examined several locations for the public event during our planning meeting. We considered stadium and school building, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose park. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"Which hobby did the speaker choose?","choices":["art palette","football","camera"],"images":["images/illustration-2-1.png","images/illustration-2-2.png","images/illustration-2-3.png"],"answer":0,"script":"I talked with my friends about a new hobby during our planning meeting. We considered football and camera, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose art palette. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"What is the family's weekend plan?","choices":["beach","camping","shopping"],"images":["images/illustration-5-1.png","images/illustration-5-2.png","images/illustration-5-3.png"],"answer":1,"script":"My family considered different plans for the weekend during our planning meeting. We considered beach and shopping, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose camping. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."},{"stem":"What is the confirmed time of the event?","choices":["3 oclock","7 oclock","5 oclock"],"images":["images/illustration-4-1.png","images/illustration-4-2.png","images/illustration-4-3.png"],"answer":2,"script":"The organisers discussed the starting time of the event during our planning meeting. Some suggested 3 o’clock, while others preferred 7 o’clock. The official notice confirms 5 o’clock. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare."}],"audio":"audio/g9-d-listening-1.mp3","transcript":"Text 1. I compared different sports before joining a school club during our planning meeting. We considered swimming and running, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose cycling. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 2. The organisers examined several locations for the public event during our planning meeting. We considered stadium and school building, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose park. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 3. I talked with my friends about a new hobby during our planning meeting. We considered football and camera, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose art palette. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 4. My family considered different plans for the weekend during our planning meeting. We considered beach and shopping, but neither matched our needs. After reviewing the options, we chose camping. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.\n\nText 5. The organisers discussed the starting time of the event during our planning meeting. Some suggested 3 o’clock, while others preferred 7 o’clock. The official notice confirms 5 o’clock. We have now shared the final decision and everyone knows what to prepare.","instructions":"Listen to five short texts. For each text, choose the correct picture (A, B or C)."},{"id":"g9-D-L2","skill":"listening","group":2,"title":"Listening 2 — Interview","marks":5,"items":[{"stem":"What did students repair first?","choices":["a bicycle","a school bus","a book shelf"],"answer":2},{"stem":"When did the workshop open?","choices":["October","May","January"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Who helps the students?","choices":["a footballer","a shop cashier","a local carpenter"],"answer":2},{"stem":"When do they meet?","choices":["Monday after lessons","Thursday morning","Saturday noon"],"answer":0},{"stem":"What have they learned?","choices":["measuring carefully","selling tickets","writing poems"],"answer":0}],"audio":"audio/g9-d-listening-2.mp3","transcript":"Interviewer: Welcome to our student voices programme. Today we are speaking with Rashid about the school repair workshop. Why was this project started? Rashid: We wanted to help our school in a practical way, not just read about problems. Students suggested ideas, and our teachers helped us organise a small team. Interviewer: Tell us about your first activity. Rashid: They first repaired a broken book shelf. We chose something manageable and listened to feedback from other students. Interviewer: When did everything begin? Rashid: The workshop opened in January. We prepared the materials together and agreed on simple responsibilities. Interviewer: Who can get involved? Rashid: A local carpenter helps the students. Everyone needs to show respect and take turns. Interviewer: When does the group meet? Rashid: They meet every Monday after lessons. We try to finish each session with a useful plan for the next one. Interviewer: What is the most important lesson? Rashid: They have learned to measure carefully before cutting wood. It has also helped us become more confident when explaining our ideas. Interviewer: Would you recommend joining? Rashid: Yes. Try something small, listen to others, and keep improving. Thank you for inviting me. We are proud that young people can lead meaningful changes, even when resources are limited and progress takes time.","instructions":"Listen to the interview twice. Choose the correct answer (A, B or C)."},{"id":"g9-D-GV","skill":"grammar_vocabulary","group":1,"title":"Grammar & Vocabulary — One cloze","marks":10,"passage":"Last Friday, our students (1) ____ a small exhibition about inventions. They (2) ____ models from recycled materials and (3) ____ how each one works. A visitor asked (4) ____ the devices could save energy. Good practical ideas need careful (5) ____. We (6) ____ a short survey to collect opinions. The results were (7) ____ than expected. Everyone was (8) ____ when families asked questions. We (9) ____ to improve the models next term and (10) ____ another exhibition.","items":[{"stem":"Gap 1","choices":["organised","organise","organising","organises"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 2","choices":["built","build","building","builds"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 3","choices":["explain","explaining","explains","explained"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 4","choices":["weather","either","whether","rather"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 5","choices":["test","tested","testing","tests"],"answer":2},{"stem":"Gap 6","choices":["conduct","conducted","conducting","conducts"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Gap 7","choices":["better","good","best","well"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 8","choices":["delighted","delight","delighting","delightful"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Gap 9","choices":["planned","planning","plans","plan"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Gap 10","choices":["hosted","hosting","host","hosts"],"answer":2}],"instructions":"Read the text. For each gap (1–10), choose the correct option (A, B, C or D)."},{"id":"g9-D-R1","skill":"reading","group":1,"title":"Reading 1 — Matching","marks":4,"passage":"Match each person (1–4) with a suitable activity (A–E). One activity is extra.","people":["Mazin regularly visits the coast with his family and worries when he sees plastic waste near the water. He wants to help protect sea animals and learn how young people can keep beaches cleaner.","Huda enjoys asking questions and writing about interesting events at school. She hopes to become a reporter and needs practice in presenting facts clearly without spreading rumours or leaving out important information.","Ali has started learning basic computer programming. He wants to create a small interactive game, but he needs guidance on choosing instructions, testing his code and finding simple mistakes.","Layla loves spending time in the kitchen with her grandparents. She wants to record traditional family recipes and understand why particular dishes are prepared for celebrations and important occasions."],"options":["This beginners' coding club teaches students to create simple animations and interactive games. Members practise giving clear instructions to a computer and work together to discover and fix errors.","Coastal volunteers visit beaches to collect litter and monitor areas used by sea animals. They learn about marine ecosystems and encourage families to prevent plastic waste from reaching the water.","The family cooking project brings young people together with older relatives. Members prepare traditional dishes, discuss local ingredients and write recipe cards to preserve family knowledge.","At the student newsroom, young reporters interview members of the school community and check their facts. They learn how to write clear, balanced articles for a magazine and online noticeboard.","Members of the cycling group practise safe journeys on local roads. They learn about bicycle maintenance, plan routes and build confidence by completing supervised rides with other students."],"items":[{"stem":"Person 1: Mazin regularly visits the coast with his family and worries when he sees plastic waste near the water. He wants to help protect sea animals and learn how young people can keep beaches cleaner.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":1},{"stem":"Person 2: Huda enjoys asking questions and writing about interesting events at school. She hopes to become a reporter and needs practice in presenting facts clearly without spreading rumours or leaving out important information.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":3},{"stem":"Person 3: Ali has started learning basic computer programming. He wants to create a small interactive game, but he needs guidance on choosing instructions, testing his code and finding simple mistakes.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":0},{"stem":"Person 4: Layla loves spending time in the kitchen with her grandparents. She wants to record traditional family recipes and understand why particular dishes are prepared for celebrations and important occasions.","choices":["A","B","C","D","E"],"answer":2}],"instructions":"Read the four short descriptions and the five activity descriptions. Match each person to an activity."},{"id":"g9-D-R2","skill":"reading","group":2,"title":"Reading 2 — Short answers","marks":6,"passage":"In a town outside Muscat, parents and students began discussing the journey to school. Heavy traffic near the main gate made mornings stressful, and some families felt that walking was unsafe. A student group counted vehicles for a week and interviewed classmates about their usual travel choices. The figures showed that many pupils lived close enough to walk, but parents worried about crossing a busy road.\n\nThe school proposed a marked walking route with volunteers supervising the busiest crossings. The municipality agreed to paint clearer road markings, while a local business donated reflective signs. Not everyone supported the plan. Some parents questioned whether volunteers could keep pupils safe in bad weather, and shop owners feared losing parking spaces. The organisers therefore introduced the route as a six-week trial instead of making a permanent change immediately.\n\nDuring the trial, teachers recorded arrival times and volunteers reported dangerous situations. The school also asked pupils to describe how they felt about the journey. More students began walking in small groups, and congestion near the gate decreased on several mornings. However, rain on two days exposed a problem: parts of the path became slippery. The organisers added temporary warning signs and asked the municipality to improve the surface before expanding the route.\n\nThe trial did not prove that walking is the best choice for every family. Some children live far away or need transport for personal reasons. Still, the project showed that collecting evidence can improve everyday decisions. The school plans to review the figures next term before asking the council for permanent changes. In my opinion, safer routes are worth exploring, provided that families have choices and safety concerns are taken seriously.","passageTitle":"A New Route for School Journeys","items":[{"stem":"What problem occurred near the school gate?","answers":["heavy traffic"]},{"stem":"How long did students count vehicles?","answers":["one week"]},{"stem":"Who donated reflective signs?","answers":["a local business"]},{"stem":"How long was the route trial?","answers":["six weeks"]},{"stem":"What problem appeared during rain?","answers":["the path became slippery"]},{"stem":"When will the school review the data?","answers":["next term"]}],"instructions":"Read the text, then answer the six questions in short answers."},{"id":"g9-D-W1","skill":"writing","group":1,"title":"Writing 1 — Story","marks":5,"prompt":"Write a story about finding an unusual object while helping someone. Explain what happened and what you learned.","minWords":100},{"id":"g9-D-W2","skill":"writing","group":2,"title":"Writing 2 — Email","marks":5,"prompt":"Write an email to a friend about a public place you visited for the first time. Describe what interested you and suggest a visit together.","minWords":100}],"allocation":"archive_previous_D","unit_id":null,"verification":"not_unit_verified"}]};
